WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.205
Yesterday my plumber asked me, before he had even packed away his tools, whether I would kindly leave him 'a little review'. He had fixed the leak in twenty minutes, with perfect courtesy; so I gave him five stars, the way one slips someone a tip. Then I received an email from the platform that had sent him: could I rate my 'booking experience'? And, hard on its heels, a text from my bank: on a scale of one to ten, would I recommend its app to a friend?

00:00:30.455 --> 00:00:58.456
Here we all are, turned into critics without having asked to be. The intention is laudable: give customers a voice, reward good tradespeople, weed out the bad ones. But the system has its perverse effects. When four stars out of five are taken as a reproach, the rating stops measuring anything at all; it becomes a ritual of politeness, even a form of emotional blackmail. The driver who confides that his job depends on his rating hardly leaves you any choice.

00:00:58.706 --> 00:01:21.144
There is something more worrying still. By dint of constant rating, service jobs are turning into permanent performances, where the smile counts as much as the work done well. And we customers are getting into the habit of judging rather than thanking. A restaurant is no longer a place where one dined well or badly, but a 'four point two' whose average one checks before pushing open the door.

00:01:21.394 --> 00:01:47.074
The system also feeds on its own inflation. Since everyone gets four point eight, the rating can no longer tell the excellent from the merely adequate, and platforms have to invent new marks of distinction: badges, labels, 'staff pick' tags. Not to mention fake reviews, written by the batch, which now oblige the authorities to hunt down what was first presented as a tool of transparency.

00:01:47.324 --> 00:02:06.756
I am not proposing to abolish reviews; some have spared me memorable disappointments. I am merely pleading for a little restraint. There is an old technique we could bring back: saying thank you, face to face, to the person concerned. It leaves no trace on the internet, but it has the merit of being heard.
